window.lessonBank = window.lessonBank || {};
Object.assign(window.lessonBank, {
  taxa1: {
    id:'taxa1', unit:'TAXA2000', unitName:'Introduction to Australian Taxation Law', date:'20–26 Jul', week:1,
    title:'Income — from receipt to net tax payable', duration:'120–170 min',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin Topic 1 Income lecture notes (66 pages), TAXA2000 program calendar and the supplied MST revision tutorial transcript.',
    objectives:[
      'Use the unit’s full assessable income → taxable income → net tax payable workflow.',
      'Separate ordinary income, statutory income, exempt income and non-assessable non-exempt income.',
      'Distinguish income from capital and identify when income is derived.',
      'Handle allowances, reimbursements, fringe benefits, dividends and franking credits.',
      'Explain cash versus accrual derivation for business and professional income.',
      'Calculate the role of Medicare levy, LITO, PAYG and the small-business tax offset in the course framework.'
    ],
    map:['identify taxpayer + year','classify receipts','assessable income','less allowable deductions','taxable income','tax + Medicare','offsets / credits','payable or refund'],
    sections:[
      {title:'1. The tax calculation is a pipeline', kicker:'big picture', body:[
        'Do not start a tax problem by reaching for a tax rate. First determine what belongs in assessable income, then subtract allowable deductions. Only then do you have taxable income — the number that the tax-rate table works on.',
        'The Curtin sequence is deliberately mechanical because it stops you mixing income rules, deduction rules and tax-payment rules. PAYG withholding and franking credits, for example, are not deductions from taxable income; they are credits used later in the calculation.'
      ], flow:['Assessable income','− deductions','Taxable income','× tax rates','Gross tax','+ Medicare','− non-refundable offsets','− refundable credits','Net payable / refund'], checkpoint:'I can write the entire calculation sequence from memory and place PAYG/franking credits in the correct stage.'},
      {title:'2. The two Income Tax Assessment Acts', kicker:'legal framework', body:[
        'The unit works across the Income Tax Assessment Act 1936 and the Income Tax Assessment Act 1997. You do not need to treat them as competing statutes. Modern provisions are spread across both Acts, so the task is to recognise the section relevant to the issue.',
        'The course financial year runs 1 July to 30 June. In the supplied slides, CY means 1 July 2025 to 30 June 2026, PY the preceding year and FY the following year.'
      ], bullets:['s 4-15: taxable income = assessable income − deductions.','s 6-5: ordinary income.','s 6-10: statutory income.','Division 6: assessable-income framework.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why a TAXA answer can legitimately cite both the 1936 and 1997 Acts.'},
      {title:'3. Ordinary income versus statutory income', kicker:'classification', body:[
        'Ordinary income is income according to ordinary concepts. Think receipts that come in as a product of labour, business, property or investment — wages, interest, rent and similar receipts. Regularity is a strong clue, but a one-off receipt can still be ordinary income in the right circumstances.',
        'Statutory income is included because a specific provision says so. The lecture examples include allowances, some reimbursements, compensation receipts, capital gains and franking credits. A good answer asks ordinary income first, then statutory income, then whether an exemption takes it back out.'
      ], flow:['ordinary income?','if no / not enough → statutory provision?','residency + source?','exempt or NANE?','final assessable amount'], checkpoint:'Given a new receipt I can explain the route I would use to decide whether it is assessable.'},
      {title:'4. Income versus capital — the tree and fruit idea', kicker:'core distinction', body:[
        'A useful course analogy is the tree and its fruit. The asset or source is the tree; recurring returns generated by it are the fruit. Selling shares held as a long-term investment is ordinarily a capital event, while dividends from those shares are income.',
        'Do not assume every one-off receipt is capital or every repeated receipt is income. The surrounding purpose and legal rule still matter. The CGT topic later deals with capital gains as statutory income.'
      ], table:[['Receipt','Typical starting character'],['Salary / wages','Ordinary income'],['Interest / rent','Ordinary income'],['Dividend','Assessable income; franking rules may also apply'],['Sale of long-term investment asset','Capital / CGT analysis'],['Windfall prize unrelated to work','Usually not income'],['Prize incidental to employment/profession','May be assessable']], checkpoint:'I can distinguish the source asset from the return produced by the source.'},
      {title:'5. When is income derived?', kicker:'timing', body:[
        'Income is taxed in the year it is derived. For salary and wages, the course uses receipt: if the employee receives the money this year, it is derived this year even when the payment relates to leave to be taken later.',
        'Receipt can be constructive. If money is dealt with at your direction rather than physically placed in your hand, it may still have been derived. Interest credited to a bank account is the standard example of receipt without cash being handed over.'
      ], bullets:['Salary and wages: when received.','Rent/royalties in the course treatment: when received.','Interest: when paid or credited to you.','Constructive receipt: dealt with on your behalf can count as received.'], callout:'The MST revision transcript specifically flags income derivation — especially cash versus accrual — as a common difficulty area.', checkpoint:'I can identify the correct tax year for a salary, interest or constructively received amount.'},
      {title:'6. Business income — cash versus accruals', kicker:'business timing', body:[
        'A trading business generally derives income when the sale is made rather than waiting for cash. That is the accruals or earnings approach. The lecture contrasts this with small personal professional practices, which can be treated on a cash/receipts basis, while larger professional practices use accruals.',
        'Prepaid service income can also depend on whether the service has actually been performed. The Arthur Murray material is used to show that money received in advance for services not yet provided may remain a liability until the relevant service is supplied.'
      ], checkpoint:'I can explain why “cash arrived” is not always the same thing as “business income was derived”.'},
      {title:'7. Exempt income and why it still matters', kicker:'excluded amounts', body:[
        'Exempt income does not enter assessable income, but it can still affect other calculations. The lecture notes that expenditure incurred in deriving exempt income is not deductible and that exempt income is relevant when dealing with carried-forward tax losses.',
        'The course gives examples such as certain entity income, specified social-security or disaster payments and reserve-force income. Always use the course materials or exam aid to confirm the category rather than guessing from whether a payment feels “governmental”.'
      ], checkpoint:'I can explain one consequence of exempt income beyond simply saying “it is not taxed”.'},
      {title:'8. Allowances, reimbursements and fringe benefits', kicker:'employment receipts', body:[
        'A cash allowance is generally paid before the employee incurs the expense and is assessable. A reimbursement generally repays the actual expense after the event and is not assessable to the employee, though fringe-benefits-tax consequences can arise for the employer.',
        'The lecture highlights kilometre-based reimbursements as an exception to the simple reimbursement rule: the amount can be assessable, with the employee separately considering the appropriate travel deduction.'
      ], table:[['Item','Employee income treatment in this course'],['Cash allowance','Generally assessable'],['Actual-cost reimbursement','Generally not assessable'],['c/km reimbursement','Assessable; consider separate deduction'],['Non-cash fringe benefit','Not assessable to employee; FBT sits with employer']], checkpoint:'I can classify an employer payment without confusing an allowance with a reimbursement.'},
      {title:'9. Dividends and franking credits — gross up, then credit', kicker:'investment income', body:[
        'A franked dividend creates two tax entries for the shareholder. The cash dividend is assessable, and the attached franking credit is also included in assessable income. Later, that same franking credit is used as a refundable tax offset.',
        'That is why “including the franking credit in income” is not double-taxing the shareholder: the credit is also recognised in the tax-payment stage.'
      ], formulas:['Non-BRE credit for a fully franked dividend = cash dividend × 30/70','BRE-style credit used in the course = cash dividend × 25/75'], checkpoint:'I can gross up a franked dividend and then put the credit in the correct later stage.'},
      {title:'10. Compensation, illegal receipts, prizes and windfalls', kicker:'edge cases', body:[
        'Compensation generally takes the character of what it replaces. Compensation replacing lost salary or business income tends to be income; compensation replacing a capital asset or for personal injury tends toward capital/non-assessable treatment under the relevant rules.',
        'The lecture also makes the point that income from illegal activity can still be assessable. By contrast, pure windfalls such as ordinary lottery/game-show prizes are generally not assessable unless the prize is sufficiently connected to employment, business or professional activity.'
      ], checkpoint:'I can analyse a compensation receipt by asking what it substitutes for.'},
      {title:'11. Medicare, offsets and credits', kicker:'after taxable income', body:[
        'Once taxable income is determined, calculate gross tax from the current course rate table. The ordinary Medicare levy in the lesson is generally 2% above applicable thresholds; the Medicare levy surcharge is specifically outside this unit’s assessed scope.',
        'Non-refundable offsets are applied before refundable credits. LITO cannot create a refund by itself; PAYG and franking credits can. This ordering is repeatedly reinforced in the lecture and revision class.'
      ], flow:['gross tax','+ Medicare levy','− LITO / SBEO','− PAYG / franking credits','net payable or refund'], checkpoint:'I can explain the difference between a deduction, a non-refundable offset and a refundable credit.'},
      {title:'12. Small Business Entity framework', kicker:'course concessions', body:[
        'The Income deck introduces the SBE framework because it reappears throughout the semester: prepayments, trading stock, depreciation, CGT concessions and the small-business tax offset. The slide deck describes the general SBE turnover threshold for these concessions as less than $10 million, while individual concessions can have their own limits.',
        'The small-business income tax offset in the course is 16% of the tax attributable to total net small-business income, capped at $1,000 and non-refundable. The lesson should be used with the current exam aid because rates and thresholds are exactly the kind of figure that can change by year.'
      ], checkpoint:'I can name at least four later topics where SBE status changes the tax treatment.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'An employee receives $36,000 this year, including $1,925 for annual leave that starts next year. How much salary is derived this year under the course approach?', answer:'$36,000. Salary and wages are derived when received; the later leave period does not defer the amount.'},
      {q:'A taxpayer receives a $5,200 franked dividend with a $300 franking credit. What goes into assessable income?', answer:'$5,500: the dividend plus the franking credit. The $300 credit is then also used later as a refundable tax offset.'},
      {q:'Why is PAYG withholding not an allowable deduction?', answer:'It is tax already paid/withheld on the taxpayer’s behalf, so it is a refundable credit in the final tax calculation, not an expense used to calculate taxable income.'},
      {q:'A business receives cash in advance for services that have not yet been performed. What issue must you test?', answer:'Derivation timing. Under the course material, prepaid service income may not be derived until the service is provided.'}
    ],
    finish:['Write the full seven-step tax calculation without notes.','Classify at least ten receipts as ordinary/statutory/exempt/capital and explain why.','Solve one dividend/franking-credit example.','Explain cash versus accrual derivation and constructive receipt.','Complete one net-tax-payable calculation using the current exam aid rates.']
  },

  taxa2: {
    id:'taxa2', unit:'TAXA2000', unitName:'Introduction to Australian Taxation Law', date:'27 Jul–9 Aug', week:'2–3',
    title:'Deductions — deciding what really reduces taxable income', duration:'170–230 min',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin Topic 2 Deductions lecture notes (74 pages), official Topic 2 answers and the supplied MST revision tutorial transcript.',
    objectives:['Apply both positive limbs and all four negative limbs of s 8-1.','Use apportionment when an expense has mixed purposes.','Distinguish repairs from improvements and initial repairs.','Apply specific deduction rules for tax, lease, borrowing, mortgage discharge, bad debts and related items.','Work through self-education, clothing, travel, gifts, entertainment and associated-person rules.','Apply prepayment, tax-loss, non-commercial-loss and trading-stock rules in the course framework.'],
    map:['expense incurred','positive limb?','negative limb?','specific provision?','apportion if mixed','timing rule?','allowable deduction'],
    sections:[
      {title:'1. s 8-1 is a decision test, not a shopping list', kicker:'big picture', body:[
        'Start with the connection between the outgoing and the income-producing activity. For non-business income, the question is whether the expense is incurred in gaining or producing assessable income. For business income, ask whether it is necessarily incurred in carrying on the business for that purpose.',
        'Then test the negative limbs. Even a work-connected expense can fail if it is capital, private/domestic, connected to exempt/NANE income, or specifically denied elsewhere in the Act.'
      ], flow:['income connection','positive limb','negative limbs','specific rule','apportionment','deduction'], checkpoint:'I can analyse a deduction from principles rather than memorising whether one example was deductible.'},
      {title:'2. “To the extent” means mixed expenses can be split', kicker:'apportionment', body:[
        'Tax deductions are not always all-or-nothing. If an expense has a genuine income-producing portion and a private portion, only the income-producing extent is deductible.',
        'The same idea reappears in phone use, home running costs, mixed travel, rental-property costs and depreciating assets. Always state the basis for the percentage rather than simply multiplying by an unsupported estimate.'
      ], checkpoint:'I can identify when apportionment is required and explain what evidence would support it.'},
      {title:'3. The four negative limbs', kicker:'what blocks s 8-1', table:[['Negative limb','Meaning','Typical course example'],['Capital','Creates/enduring asset or structural advantage','purchase of plant, major improvement'],['Private/domestic','Personal or household character','childcare, ordinary clothing, private home costs'],['Exempt/NANE connection','Expense incurred to derive excluded income','cost of deriving exempt income'],['Specifically denied','Another provision prevents deduction','fines, many entertainment costs']], checkpoint:'I can state all four negatives before moving to a specific deduction provision.'},
      {title:'4. Clothing, uniforms and associated cleaning', kicker:'employee expenses', body:[
        'Conventional clothing is ordinarily private, even when an employer expects a certain appearance. The course allows deductions for compulsory uniforms, protective clothing and occupation-specific clothing, with special rules for registered non-compulsory uniforms.',
        'When the clothing itself is deductible, associated cleaning and repair costs can also be deductible. This is why “dry cleaning” cannot be answered without first classifying the clothing.'
      ], checkpoint:'I can explain why a compulsory uniform and an ordinary business suit are treated differently.'},
      {title:'5. Work/home travel and home-study costs', kicker:'location matters', body:[
        'Ordinary home-to-work travel is generally private. The lecture lists exceptions such as a home that is genuinely the place of business, required carriage of bulky equipment when no storage is available, travel between workplaces, or situations where work genuinely begins at home.',
        'Home running costs used for work may be deductible to the relevant extent; a self-employed taxpayer can have broader occupancy-cost issues, with possible CGT consequences.'
      ], checkpoint:'I can explain why changing the starting/ending location changes the travel result.'},
      {title:'6. Specific deductions — know when the Act rescues a capital-looking cost', kicker:'Division 25', body:[
        'Some expenses fail the general deduction test but are made deductible by a specific provision. The course therefore expects you to recognise the relevant section and apply its conditions, not stop at “capital”.'
      ], table:[['Provision','Course use'],['s 25-5','tax-related expenses'],['s 25-10','repairs'],['s 25-20','lease-document expenses'],['s 25-25','borrowing expenses'],['s 25-30','mortgage-discharge expenses'],['s 25-35','bad debts'],['s 25-45','theft/embezzlement losses'],['s 25-50','certain pensions/retiring allowances'],['s 25-55','payments to associations']], checkpoint:'I can match the common specific-deduction issue to the section used in this unit.'},
      {title:'7. Repairs versus improvements and initial repairs', kicker:'s 25-10', body:[
        'A repair restores something to its former condition rather than replacing or upgrading the whole asset. Painting, mending leaks and restoring worn parts can fit this idea. A substantial modification or improvement that enhances the asset is capital.',
        'Initial repairs are a classic trap. If a defect already existed when an income-producing property was acquired, fixing it shortly after purchase is generally capital rather than a deductible repair. Damage arising during the taxpayer’s income-producing ownership is different.'
      ], checkpoint:'I can distinguish an initial repair, a genuine repair and a capital improvement from facts.'},
      {title:'8. Borrowing expenses — daily rate over the correct period', kicker:'s 25-25', body:[
        'Borrowing expenses are capital in nature but are specifically deductible. The lecture treats amounts over $100 as spread over the lesser of the loan term, the actual period if repaid early, or five years.',
        'The revision tutorial stresses the calculation method: calculate a daily rate to two decimal places, then multiply by the number of relevant days in the financial year.'
      ], formulas:['daily rate = total borrowing expense ÷ total deductible-period days','year deduction = daily rate × relevant days in that year'], checkpoint:'I can build a borrowing-expense schedule across years and handle an early repayment.'},
      {title:'9. Mortgage discharge, bad debts and theft', kicker:'specific provisions', body:[
        'A mortgage-discharge expense can be deductible where the mortgage secured money borrowed for income-earning purposes. A bad debt generally needs to be genuinely bad, written off in the year claimed and previously brought to account as income.',
        'Losses through theft, embezzlement or misappropriation by an employee can be specifically deductible where the statutory conditions are met.'
      ], checkpoint:'I can identify why a mortgage discharge fee may be deductible even though the mortgage itself is capital financing.'},
      {title:'10. Payments to associated persons', kicker:'reasonableness', body:[
        'You can employ or pay an associate, but s 26-35 limits the deduction to the amount the Commissioner considers reasonable when the payment is excessive. The tutorial deliberately uses a “girlfriend” trick: not every close personal relationship automatically falls within the course examples of an associated person.',
        'If the person is an associate and $30/hour is paid when $20/hour is reasonable, the deduction is based on the reasonable amount rather than automatically the cash paid.'
      ], checkpoint:'I can separate “was cash paid?” from “how much is tax-deductible?” for an associate.'},
      {title:'11. Self-education', kicker:'current income connection', body:[
        'Self-education can be deductible when it maintains or improves skills used in the taxpayer’s current income-earning activity or is likely to increase income in that existing activity. It is generally too soon when the study is undertaken to obtain initial employment, new employment or open a new income-earning activity.',
        'HECS-HELP is specifically non-deductible to the student in this course. Other costs such as textbooks, stationery, eligible travel and decline in value of assets may be considered if the core connection exists.'
      ], callout:'The revision class uses travel patterns involving home, study and work. When all three locations are involved, the first leg can be deductible while the second can be private, depending on the pattern taught in the course diagrams.', checkpoint:'I can first decide whether the course itself is connected to current work before analysing individual study expenses.'},
      {title:'12. Prepayments — the $1,000 gate and SBE 12-month rule', kicker:'timing', body:[
        'A prepayment pays now for something to be done in a later income year. Amounts under $1,000 are excluded from the prepayment spreading rules in the course. When the rules apply, expenditure is apportioned over the eligible service period, capped at ten years.',
        'For an SBE that chooses the concession, an otherwise deductible prepayment can be immediately deducted when the eligible service period is 12 months or less and ends by the end of the following income year. Otherwise it is apportioned. The revision tutorial identifies this as one of the highest-risk calculation areas.'
      ], formulas:['apportioned deduction = prepayment × relevant days ÷ eligible service period days'], checkpoint:'I can decide whether to deduct immediately or apportion before doing any arithmetic.'},
      {title:'13. Gifts, entertainment, fines and other denied items', kicker:'specific exclusions', body:[
        'A gift generally needs to be at least $2 and made to a deductible gift recipient. Buying a raffle ticket is not a gift because the payer receives a chance to win something in return.',
        'Fines and penalties are not deductible. Entertainment is generally denied unless an exception in the course applies, such as certain employer, seminar, promotional or entertainment-business circumstances.'
      ], checkpoint:'I can explain why generosity or business purpose alone does not automatically make an amount deductible.'},
      {title:'14. Tax losses and non-commercial losses', kicker:'when deductions exceed income', body:[
        'When allowable deductions exceed assessable income, a tax loss can generally be carried forward. Net exempt income affects the amount of prior loss available for deduction. Some deductions — including gifts, certain personal super contributions and some retiring allowances — cannot create or increase a tax loss.',
        'Division 35 quarantines certain losses from non-commercial activities unless one of the course tests is met: assessable income, profits, real property or other assets.'
      ], table:[['Div 35 test','Course threshold/idea'],['Assessable income','activity income > $20,000'],['Profits','profit in 3 of last 5 years'],['Real property','relevant property > $500,000'],['Other assets','relevant other assets > $100,000, excluding passenger vehicle']], checkpoint:'I can distinguish a carried-forward tax loss from a quarantined non-commercial activity loss.'},
      {title:'15. Trading stock', kicker:'Div 70', body:[
        'Trading stock covers items produced, manufactured or acquired for manufacture, sale or exchange in the ordinary course of business. Gross profit can be understood through sales less cost of goods sold, where COGS uses opening stock + purchases/production − closing stock.',
        'Stock on hand can be valued using cost, market value or replacement value. The slides accept FIFO, weighted-average and standard-cost approaches and reject LIFO/base-stock methods. An SBE can choose not to account for a year-end stock change when the difference between opening and closing value is less than $5,000.'
      ], formulas:['COGS = opening stock + purchases/production − closing stock','Gross profit = sales − COGS'], checkpoint:'I can calculate the stock adjustment and explain whether it becomes assessable income or a deduction.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'A rental-property mortgage discharge fee of $480 is paid. The mortgage secured borrowing used to produce rental income. What provision is the course looking for?', answer:'s 25-30 mortgage-discharge expenses; the course treats the fee as specifically deductible in this income-producing context.'},
      {q:'Borrowing expenses are $2,500 on a 10-year loan. What maximum spreading period does the course use if the loan stays on foot?', answer:'Five years, because the deduction is spread over the lesser of loan term/actual period/five years.'},
      {q:'A $1,500 prepayment is made by a non-SBE and spans two income years. What should you do first?', answer:'Determine the eligible service period and apportion the otherwise deductible amount over the relevant days rather than claiming all $1,500 immediately.'},
      {q:'A taxpayer buys $50 of raffle tickets from a DGR charity. Is $50 a deductible gift?', answer:'No. A raffle-ticket purchase gives something in return — the chance to win — and is not treated as a deductible gift in the course.'},
      {q:'Opening stock is $3,000, purchases $5,000, closing stock $6,000 and sales $10,000. Gross profit?', answer:'COGS = 3,000 + 5,000 − 6,000 = 2,000; gross profit = 10,000 − 2,000 = $8,000.'}
    ],
    finish:['Apply s 8-1 positive and negative limbs to ten fresh expenses.','Complete one repairs/initial-repairs classification set.','Calculate borrowing expenses and a prepayment over days.','Work a self-education/travel problem.','Calculate a tax loss and trading-stock adjustment.','Check Topic 2 official answers only after attempting comparable questions yourself.']
  },

  taxa3: {
    id:'taxa3', unit:'TAXA2000', unitName:'Introduction to Australian Taxation Law', date:'10–16 Aug', week:4,
    title:'Uniform Capital Allowances — depreciation, pools and capital works', duration:'140–190 min',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin Topic 3 Uniform Capital Allowances lecture notes (47 pages) plus the supplied MST revision tutorial transcript.',
    objectives:['Identify a depreciating asset and determine its cost, effective life and start time.','Calculate prime-cost and diminishing-value decline in value.','Handle taxable/private-use apportionment and balancing adjustments.','Apply non-business immediate write-off and low-value-pool rules taught in the module.','Apply SBE instant asset write-off and general-pool rules for 2025–26.','Calculate Division 43 capital-works deductions and recognise the CGT interaction.'],
    map:['depreciating asset?','cost','effective life','start time','pool?','prime cost / diminishing value','adjustments / disposal','Div 43 for buildings'],
    sections:[
      {title:'1. Depreciation is a tax deduction for decline in value', kicker:'big picture', body:['Division 40 allows a deduction for the decline in value of a depreciating asset used for a taxable purpose. A depreciating asset has a limited effective life and can reasonably be expected to decline in value as it is used. Land and trading stock are excluded from this definition.','If use is partly private, the deduction is reduced to the taxable-use proportion. The tax calculation therefore separates the asset’s economic decline from the percentage connected to assessable income.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why buying an asset is not usually an immediate s 8-1 deduction but its decline in value may be deductible.'},
      {title:'2. The seven-step Schedule A logic', kicker:'workflow', flow:['1 depreciating asset','2 cost','3 effective life','4 start time','5 pooling decision','6 calculate decline','7 disposal/adjustments'], body:['The safest way to solve a depreciation question is to follow this order. It prevents common mistakes such as using the purchase date instead of the first-use date, forgetting private-use apportionment or performing a normal depreciation calculation on an asset that should be pooled.'], checkpoint:'I can reproduce the seven-step sequence without notes.'},
      {title:'3. Cost — first and second elements', kicker:'step 2', body:['First-element cost is normally what you paid to acquire the asset. Second-element costs are amounts paid after acquisition that contribute to the asset’s present condition or location, such as qualifying freight, installation, alterations or improvements.','These costs matter because the depreciation formulas use cost/base value, not merely the invoice price.' ], checkpoint:'I can decide whether an amount belongs in the depreciating asset’s tax cost.'},
      {title:'4. Effective life and start time', kicker:'steps 3–4', body:['Effective life is the period over which the asset can be used to earn assessable income. The course allows the Commissioner’s effective life or a self-assessed life where permitted.','Start time is when the asset is first used or installed ready for use, not automatically the day it is ordered or paid for. The number of days in the first year runs from that start time.'], checkpoint:'I can identify which date controls first-year depreciation.'},
      {title:'5. Diminishing value', kicker:'accelerated deduction', body:['Diminishing value applies the rate to a base value that reduces over time. This produces a larger deduction earlier and smaller deductions as the adjustable value falls. The revision tutorial says to default to diminishing value in its worked asset example when the taxpayer is assumed to want the larger earlier deduction, unless the question specifies otherwise.'], formulas:['Decline in value = base value × days/365 × (200% ÷ effective life)','closing adjustable value = opening/base value + second-element costs − decline in value'], checkpoint:'I can calculate the rate, first-year days and closing adjustable value.'},
      {title:'6. Prime cost', kicker:'straight-line pattern', body:['Prime cost applies the rate to cost rather than a declining opening value, producing an even annual deduction for a full year over the effective life.'], formulas:['Decline in value = cost × days/365 × (100% ÷ effective life)'], checkpoint:'I can explain why prime-cost deductions stay level while diminishing-value deductions fall.'},
      {title:'7. Balancing adjustments on disposal', kicker:'step 7', body:['When a depreciating asset is disposed of, compare termination value with the adjustable value immediately before disposal. If termination value is higher, the excess is included in assessable income; if lower, the shortfall can be deductible.','This is separate from the ordinary CGT treatment of buildings and other non-depreciating CGT assets.'], checkpoint:'I can identify whether a disposal produces assessable balancing income or a deduction.'},
      {title:'8. Non-business $300 immediate deduction', kicker:'special rule', body:['The lecture includes an immediate deduction for certain assets costing $300 or less used predominantly to derive non-business assessable income, subject to the set/identical-asset restrictions. It does not apply to assets used in carrying on a business.'], checkpoint:'I can distinguish the non-business $300 rule from the SBE instant asset write-off.'},
      {title:'9. Low-value pool for non-SBE taxpayers', kicker:'pooling', body:['The UCA low-value pool can contain eligible low-cost assets and low-value assets. A low-cost asset costs less than $1,000; a low-value asset has an opening adjustable value below $1,000 and was previously depreciated using diminishing value.','Once a low-cost asset election is made, later low-cost assets must generally enter the pool. Pooled assets stay in the pool.'], formulas:['new low-cost assets: 18.75%','opening pool balance: 37.5%','low-value assets newly allocated: 37.5%'], checkpoint:'I can determine the correct pool rate for an opening balance versus a new low-cost asset.'},
      {title:'10. SBE simplified depreciation — course year 2025–26', kicker:'Subdivision 328', body:['For the year ended 30 June 2026, the supplied Curtin material uses a $20,000 instant asset write-off threshold for eligible SBE assets. Assets costing more than the threshold enter the small-business general pool.','The pool rate taught is 15% for assets added during the year and 30% for existing pooled assets. These are course-year figures: use the current exam aid if a future assessment gives a different year.'], table:[['SBE item','Course treatment'],['eligible asset < $20,000','100% immediate write-off'],['new asset added to general pool','15%'],['existing general-pool balance','30%']], checkpoint:'I can route an SBE asset to immediate write-off or the correct general-pool rate.'},
      {title:'11. Intellectual property', kicker:'special effective lives', body:['Patents, copyright, registered designs and in-house software can be intangible depreciating assets with statutory effective lives. The lecture requires the prime-cost method for the intellectual-property examples.'], checkpoint:'I can recognise that not every depreciating asset is physical plant.'},
      {title:'12. Division 43 capital works', kicker:'buildings are different', body:['Division 43 deals with qualifying construction expenditure on buildings, extensions, alterations, improvements and renovations used to produce income. The deduction is based on construction cost — not the property’s purchase price — and starts when the building is first used for an income-producing purpose.','The rate depends on construction timing and building type. In the supplied material the working rates encountered are 2.5% and 4%, and the revision tutorial tells you to use the course two-way rate table rather than guessing.'], formulas:['capital-works deduction = construction cost × Div 43 rate × eligible days/365'], callout:'Construction cost + construction date in an exam question is a strong signal that Division 43 is being tested.', checkpoint:'I can distinguish “depreciating asset under Div 40” from “building/capital works under Div 43”.'},
      {title:'13. Division 43 later changes the CGT cost base', kicker:'cross-topic link', body:['A capital-works deduction is enjoyed during ownership, but the CGT rules later prevent a double benefit. The course material therefore reduces the building’s CGT cost base/reduced cost base by Division 43 amounts that have been or could be deducted where the rule applies.','This is why UCA and CGT are taught back-to-back: a rental-property question can require both annual deductions and a later disposal calculation.'], checkpoint:'I can explain the double-benefit problem the Div 43 cost-base adjustment is preventing.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'A dishwasher costing $8,000 is first used in a rental property on 1 January and has a 7-year effective life. Which method produced the larger first-year deduction in the revision tutorial?', answer:'Diminishing value. The tutorial uses 200% ÷ 7 and 181/365 for the first-year period.'},
      {q:'An SBE buys an eligible asset for $18,000 during the 2025–26 course year. What treatment does the supplied lecture use?', answer:'The $20,000 instant asset write-off threshold applies, so the eligible asset is written off immediately.'},
      {q:'A residential rental building has a qualifying construction cost of $150,000 and the rate table gives 2.5%. It is held for only 181 days this year. What structure should the calculation use?', answer:'$150,000 × 2.5% × 181/365.'},
      {q:'Termination value exceeds adjustable value on disposal of a depreciating asset. Deduction or assessable income?', answer:'Assessable balancing adjustment income for the excess.'}
    ],
    finish:['Complete a full Schedule A example under both methods.','Complete one low-value-pool schedule.','Route several SBE assets through instant write-off/general pool.','Calculate one balancing adjustment.','Calculate and apportion a Division 43 deduction.','Explain the later CGT cost-base interaction.']
  },

  taxa4: {
    id:'taxa4', unit:'TAXA2000', unitName:'Introduction to Australian Taxation Law', date:'24 Aug–6 Sep', week:'6–7',
    title:'Capital Gains Tax — events, cost base, exemptions and discounts', duration:'170–240 min',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin Topic 4 CGT lecture notes (55 pages), PIAT Topic 4 question set and the supplied MST revision tutorial transcript.',
    objectives:['Identify whether a CGT event occurs and whether an exemption applies.','Calculate capital gain, capital loss and no-gain/no-loss outcomes using cost base and reduced cost base.','Build the five cost-base elements and apply the no-double-deduction rule.','Apply the net-capital-gain method statement in the correct order.','Handle main-residence rules, general discount and the SBE active-asset reduction taught in the unit.','Apply CGT event A1 and F1, including timing and lease-premium treatment.'],
    map:['CGT event?','exemption?','proceeds vs CB / RCB','classify gain/loss','apply losses','general discount','SBE concession','net capital gain → assessable income'],
    sections:[
      {title:'1. CGT is not a separate tax', kicker:'big picture', body:['A capital gain is statutory income. The CGT rules calculate the net capital gain, which is then added to other assessable income and taxed under the taxpayer’s ordinary rate structure.','Capital losses are ring-fenced: they reduce capital gains, not salary, rent or other ordinary income. Unused capital losses can be carried forward.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why a $10,000 capital loss cannot simply be subtracted from wages.'},
      {title:'2. The 20 September 1985 dividing line', kicker:'pre-CGT / post-CGT', body:['The modern CGT regime applies to assets acquired on or after 20 September 1985. A genuinely pre-CGT long-term investment asset can therefore sit outside CGT, though separate ordinary/statutory income provisions may still catch a profit-making scheme.','The lecture’s Gosnells land example illustrates that “pre-CGT” does not automatically mean “tax-free”: if the land was acquired with a profit-making intention, a different provision can assess the profit.'], checkpoint:'I can distinguish “CGT does not apply” from “no tax provision can possibly apply”.'},
      {title:'3. The core event test', kicker:'s 100-15 workflow', flow:['Did a CGT event happen?','Does an exemption apply?','proceeds > cost base? → gain','reduced cost base > proceeds? → loss','otherwise neither'], checkpoint:'I can use cost base for a gain and reduced cost base for a loss without mixing them.'},
      {title:'4. CGT event A1 — disposal', kicker:'the main event in this unit', body:['CGT event A1 covers disposal of a CGT asset. The timing is generally the contract date, not settlement. That can put the gain into a different financial year from the cash settlement.','CGT assets include property and legal/equitable rights: land, buildings, shares, units, options, debts and contractual rights are course examples.'], checkpoint:'I can identify the CGT year from contract timing.'},
      {title:'5. Capital proceeds and the five cost-base elements', kicker:'build the calculation', table:[['Element','What it captures'],['1','money/market value given to acquire the asset'],['2','incidental acquisition or disposal costs'],['3','certain non-capital ownership costs'],['4','capital expenditure increasing/preserving asset value'],['5','capital expenditure to establish, preserve or defend title']], checkpoint:'I can classify stamp duty, sale advertising, initial repairs, improvements and title-defence legal fees into the correct element.'},
      {title:'6. Reduced cost base and the “middle zone”', kicker:'loss calculation', body:['The reduced cost base excludes third-element ownership costs. If proceeds are below cost base but still above reduced cost base, the taxpayer has neither a capital gain nor a capital loss.','That middle zone is a common multiple-choice trap because students assume every sale below cost base creates a capital loss.'], checkpoint:'I can recognise a no-gain/no-loss outcome.'},
      {title:'7. No double deductions', kicker:'cross-topic logic', body:['If an amount has been or can be deducted elsewhere, the CGT cost-base rules generally prevent the same expenditure from also increasing cost base. Rental-property interest and rates that were deducted annually are therefore different from private ownership costs that were not deductible and may qualify for cost-base treatment.','Division 43 amounts also interact with cost base when a building is disposed of.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why the same dollar should not produce both an annual deduction and a larger CGT cost base.'},
      {title:'8. Net capital gain — order matters', kicker:'method statement', body:['First reduce current-year gains by current-year capital losses, then apply unapplied prior-year capital losses. Only after losses do you apply the general CGT discount to eligible discount gains. Finally apply applicable small-business concessions.','For the lowest net gain, the course repeatedly tells you to use losses against non-discount gains first, preserving discountable gains for the percentage reduction.'], flow:['current gains','− current losses (non-discount first)','− prior losses','apply 50% discount to eligible gains','apply SBE concession if eligible','net capital gain'], checkpoint:'I can perform the steps in order without applying a discount too early.'},
      {title:'9. Exempt assets and receipts', kicker:'s 118 examples', body:['The lecture includes common exemptions such as cars/motorcycles, certain valour decorations, assets used to derive exempt income, trading stock, specified personal-injury compensation, gambling wins and the main residence.','Do not infer an exemption just because the underlying asset is personal; identify the course exemption or main-residence rule.'], checkpoint:'I can identify the exemption before wasting time building a cost base.'},
      {title:'10. Main residence — full, absence and partial rules', kicker:'dwelling', body:['A gain on an individual’s dwelling can be disregarded where it was the taxpayer’s main residence throughout ownership. The absence rule can allow continued main-residence treatment while absent — including up to six years when the dwelling is used to produce income, subject to the course conditions.','Where the main-residence treatment covers only part of ownership or part of the property is used for business, the gain may need to be apportioned. A special market-value rule can apply when a former main residence is first used to produce assessable income.'], formulas:['partial gain = capital gain × non-main-residence days ÷ ownership days'], checkpoint:'I can decide whether the facts point to full exemption, absence rule, market-value reset or apportionment.'},
      {title:'11. General CGT discount', kicker:'Division 115', body:['The course discount is 50% for individuals and trusts, 33⅓% for complying super funds and nil for companies. The asset generally needs to be held for more than 12 months and disposed of after the discount regime commenced.','Capital losses reduce the nominal gain before the discount. Applying 50% first and then losses is a classic wrong order.'], checkpoint:'I can state entity eligibility and timing before applying a percentage.'},
      {title:'12. SBE 50% active-asset reduction', kicker:'Division 152 course scope', body:['The unit only assesses the 50% active-asset reduction from the wider suite of small-business CGT concessions. The lecture’s eligibility framework includes the small-business/net-asset gateway and requires the asset to be active — used or held ready for use in the business, or a relevant business intangible such as goodwill.','Where an individual qualifies for both the general discount and active-asset reduction, the percentage reductions can apply sequentially after losses, producing a much smaller remaining gain. Use the course/exam-aid thresholds given for the assessment year.'], checkpoint:'I can distinguish a passive investment asset from an active business asset.'},
      {title:'13. Division 43 on sale of buildings', kicker:'UCA link', body:['Buildings are not disposed of through a Division 40 balancing adjustment in the same way as depreciating plant. Instead the CGT calculation deals with the building and adjusts the cost base/reduced cost base for Division 43 deductions where required.'], checkpoint:'I can identify whether a disposal belongs in Div 40 balancing adjustment or CGT with Div 43 adjustment.'},
      {title:'14. CGT event F1 — lease premium', kicker:'special event', body:['When a lessor receives a premium for granting a lease, CGT event F1 can create a capital gain equal to the premium less relevant expenses of granting the lease. The asset is created at the time of granting the lease, so the general 50% discount is not available in the course treatment.','Do not confuse the one-off lease premium with monthly rent. Rent remains ordinary income; the premium is the F1 capital-gain issue.'], formulas:['F1 capital gain = lease premium − relevant grant-of-lease costs'], checkpoint:'I can separate a lease premium from rent and explain why the discount is unavailable.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'An individual sells shares held for 15 months. Nominal gain $12,000 and no losses. Discount gain?', answer:'$6,000 after the 50% individual discount, assuming no exclusion prevents the discount.'},
      {q:'A taxpayer has $10,000 non-discount gains, $15,140 discount gains and $10,400 capital losses. Where should the losses go first?', answer:'Against the $10,000 non-discount gains first, then the remaining $400 against discount gains, before applying the 50% discount to what remains.'},
      {q:'A lease premium is $25,000 and grant-of-lease legal costs are $2,000. Course F1 gain?', answer:'$23,000, with no general 50% discount for F1.'},
      {q:'Proceeds are $9,500, cost base $10,000 and reduced cost base $9,000. Gain/loss?', answer:'Neither. Proceeds are below cost base but above reduced cost base.'},
      {q:'A rental property was bought in 1984 as a long-term income investment and sold now. First CGT thought?', answer:'It is pre-CGT, so the modern CGT regime generally does not apply to the disposal; still consider whether another income provision could apply on different facts.'}
    ],
    finish:['Complete a five-element cost-base question.','Complete a gain/loss/no-result set using CB and RCB.','Run the full net-capital-gain method statement with mixed gains/losses.','Solve a main-residence/absence-rule problem.','Solve an active-asset and F1 example.','Attempt several PIAT Topic 4 questions before checking any later solutions.']
  },

  taxaMST: {
    id:'taxaMST', unit:'TAXA2000', unitName:'Introduction to Australian Taxation Law', date:'31 Aug–25 Sep', week:'7–10',
    title:'Mid-Semester Test Revision — Topics 1–4 under exam conditions', duration:'150–210 min',
    sourceBasis:'Supplied TAXA2000 MST tutorial transcript plus Topics 1–4 lecture materials. The transcript is a revision class, not the missing Trust lecture.',
    objectives:['Know exactly what the MST covers and how it is delivered.','Prioritise the specific trouble spots identified by the tutor.','Practise one-question-at-a-time MCQ reasoning without carrying facts across questions.','Use the Exam Aids Guidebook, physical notes and tables efficiently.','Complete a timed mixed-topic revision set.'],
    map:['Income','Deductions','UCA / Div 43','CGT','25 independent MCQs','2 hours','check calculations + wording'],
    sections:[
      {title:'1. Test format and logistics', kicker:'official tutorial briefing', body:['The supplied revision transcript states that the MST is Friday 25 September at 12:00 PM Perth time, online in Respondus/LockDown Browser, with two hours for the test plus setup/identification time. It is worth 25% and contains 25 one-mark questions.','Each multiple-choice question stands on its own even though the test uses case-study style facts. Do not waste time carrying a previous question’s result into the next unless the current question explicitly gives that information again.'], checkpoint:'I know the date, time, duration, weighting and independent-question structure.'},
      {title:'2. Permitted materials and technical preparation', kicker:'do this before test day', body:['The tutor says the electronic Exam Aids Guidebook can be accessed in the platform. The PIAT textbook and other course materials/notes need to be physical if you want to use them. The transcript strongly recommends completing the Respondus practice test in advance.','Know where to find tax rates, Medicare thresholds/rates, LITO, Division 43 rates, depreciation schedules/effective lives and the days table. A resource you cannot navigate quickly is barely a resource under time pressure.'], checkpoint:'I can locate every rate/table the tutor explicitly flagged within seconds.'},
      {title:'3. Income danger zone', kicker:'Topic 1', body:['The tutor highlights derivation as the common difficulty: when is income received/earned under cash versus accrual treatment? Salary is taxed when received in the tutorial example. Franked dividends require both cash dividend and franking credit in assessable income, with the credit used again later as a refundable offset.'], checkpoint:'I can answer timing and franking questions without confusing assessable income with tax credits.'},
      {title:'4. Deductions danger zone', kicker:'Topic 2', body:['Borrowing expenses and prepayments are identified as the two calculation-heavy deduction areas. Know the five-year-or-shorter borrowing-expense rule and daily-rate method. Know the $1,000 prepayment threshold/exclusion and the SBE 12-month rule.','Also revise specific deductions and traps: mortgage discharge, initial repairs, associated-person limits, raffle tickets, self-education and study travel.'], checkpoint:'I can recognise the calculation type before reaching for numbers.'},
      {title:'5. UCA danger zone', kicker:'Topic 3', body:['Be able to move between prime cost and diminishing value, calculate the rate from effective life, apportion for days and read a Schedule A. For the course year, remember the SBE $20,000 instant asset write-off and the 15% new / 30% existing general-pool rates.','Division 43 is the “easy mark people forget”: construction cost plus construction date should trigger the capital-works rate table.'], checkpoint:'I can recognise Div 40, SBE pooling and Div 43 as three distinct paths.'},
      {title:'6. CGT danger zone', kicker:'Topic 4', body:['Know A1 disposal and F1 lease premium. For each asset determine the gain or loss, then summarise discount gains, other/non-discount gains and losses. Apply losses to non-discount gains first, then discount gains, then reductions.','Know the pre-CGT date, general-discount holding-period rule, active-asset concept and the fact that F1 does not receive the general discount in this unit.'], checkpoint:'I can reproduce the CGT order of operations from memory.'},
      {title:'7. Tutor’s worked traps', kicker:'what students get wrong', bullets:['Annual leave paid now but taken next year: salary is still derived when received.','Franking credit: assessable income AND refundable credit.','HECS-HELP: not deductible to student.','Study travel: classify the route, not merely the purpose “university”.','Mortgage discharge on income-producing property: specific deduction can apply.','Girlfriend versus spouse/associate: do not assume association from informality.','Raffle ticket: not a deductible gift.','Holiday house: no income-producing Division 43 deduction.','Rental building: Div 43 uses construction cost/date and days held/used.','F1 lease premium: non-discountable gain.','Pre-CGT purchase: stop the ordinary CGT calculation unless another provision is relevant.','Cost-base items: distinguish deducted holding costs from capital/initial-repair/incidental items.'], checkpoint:'I can explain the reason behind each trap, not just memorise its answer.'},
      {title:'8. Time strategy for 25 questions', kicker:'exam execution', body:['Two hours for 25 questions is an average of about 4.8 minutes each, but the tutor warns that some will be near-instant true/false items while others need calculations. Bank easy marks first, flag longer calculations and leave enough time to re-read wording and check arithmetic.','The transcript repeatedly stresses careful reading and avoiding silly calculation errors. In tax, one word such as “construction”, “received”, “initial”, “private”, “held for 12 months” or “associated” can completely change the route.'], checkpoint:'I have a plan for quick questions, calculations, flagged items and final review.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'MST has 25 questions and 120 minutes. What is the raw average time per question?', answer:'4.8 minutes, but do not force equal time — quick items should take less so calculations can take more.'},
      {q:'Which four content areas are examinable?', answer:'Income, Deductions, Uniform Capital Allowances/Depreciation, and Capital Gains Tax.'},
      {q:'Are Partnerships examinable in this MST?', answer:'No. The Week 8 announcement and revision transcript both separate Partnerships from the Topics 1–4 MST scope.'},
      {q:'What should “construction cost + construction date” trigger?', answer:'Check Division 43 capital-works treatment and the course rate table.'},
      {q:'What is the first destination for capital losses when both non-discount and discount gains exist?', answer:'Non-discount gains first, then discount gains.'}
    ],
    finish:['Run the Respondus practice test.','Prepare physical notes/text resources and tab them.','Complete a mixed 25-question timed set.','Rework every error by identifying the rule that should have triggered.','Know rate/table locations without searching blindly.']
  },

  taxa5: {
    id:'taxa5', unit:'TAXA2000', unitName:'Introduction to Australian Taxation Law', date:'7–13 Sep', week:8,
    title:'Partnerships — calculate the entity’s net income, then tax the partners', duration:'110–155 min',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin Topic 5 Partnerships lecture notes (23 pages), tax-reconciliation slides and Week 8 announcement.',
    objectives:['Determine whether a tax partnership exists.','Calculate partnership net income under s 90 and separate it from accounting profit.','Explain why the partnership lodges but partners bear the income-tax consequences.','Handle partner salaries, capital interest, drawings, loans, superannuation, dividends and capital gains.','Build the partnership distribution schedule used in the Curtin material.'],
    map:['partnership exists?','calculate s90 net income','remove partner-only/accounting items','distribute under agreement','partners include shares in own returns','flow-through CGT/franking'],
    sections:[
      {title:'1. Partnership tax is a flow-through system', kicker:'big picture', body:['A partnership calculates and reports its net income, but the partnership itself does not pay income tax in its own right. The partners are assessed on their shares of partnership net income or allowed their shares of partnership loss.','That means you need two layers: first calculate the partnership amount correctly; then determine each partner’s share and partner-level consequences.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why “partnership profit” and “partner taxable income” are not the same calculation.'},
      {title:'2. What counts as a partnership for tax?', kicker:'wider than common law', body:['Common law focuses on people carrying on business in common with a view to profit. The tax definition is wider and can include people who jointly receive income, such as spouses jointly owning a rental property, even without a formal business partnership.','TR 94/8 factors in the slides include joint ownership of business assets, sharing net profits, a joint business bank account, power to operate the account, joint business name/trading, records/minutes and public recognition by customers/suppliers/creditors.'], checkpoint:'I can identify evidence for and against a partnership rather than relying only on a written partnership agreement.'},
      {title:'3. s 90 net income', kicker:'entity calculation', body:['The course defines partnership net income broadly as assessable income calculated as if the partnership were a resident taxpayer, less allowable deductions. Partner superannuation and carried-forward losses are not deductions in this partnership net-income calculation; those are dealt with at partner level.'], formulas:['Partnership net income = partnership assessable income − partnership allowable deductions'], checkpoint:'I can explain which amounts belong inside the s90 calculation and which wait for the partner.'},
      {title:'4. Partnership lodges; partners pay', kicker:'s 91 / s 92', body:['The partnership must lodge a return even though it is not itself taxed on the net income. Each partner includes their share of the partnership net income; a net partnership loss flows through as the partner’s share of the loss, subject to the broader tax rules.'], checkpoint:'I can identify who lodges and who actually bears the income tax.'},
      {title:'5. Drawings are not salary expense', kicker:'distribution mechanics', body:['A drawing is simply cash taken out in advance of the partner’s final entitlement. It does not determine the amount on which the partner is assessed. If a partner draws $50,000 but their final share of partnership profit is $80,000, they are assessed on the $80,000 share.'], checkpoint:'I can separate cash withdrawals from taxable profit allocation.'},
      {title:'6. Partner salaries and interest on capital', kicker:'not ordinary partnership deductions', body:['A “salary” paid to a partner is part of the way partnership profit is distributed, not an employee salary deduction of the partnership. Likewise, interest on partners’ capital is treated as a distribution mechanism rather than a partnership deduction.','Interest charged on drawings reduces the partner’s distribution share rather than becoming partnership assessable income.'], checkpoint:'I can reverse partner salary/capital-interest items when reconciling accounting profit to tax net income.'},
      {title:'7. Genuine loans from partners', kicker:'different from capital', body:['Where a partner separately lends money to the partnership on arm’s-length terms and the funds are used for income-producing purposes, the interest can be deductible to the partnership and assessable as interest income to the partner.','The key is to distinguish a genuine loan from the partner’s capital account or profit distribution.'], checkpoint:'I can identify when “interest paid to a partner” may actually be deductible.'},
      {title:'8. The Curtin distribution schedule', kicker:'always distribute net income', body:['The Cakes Supreme example recalculates accounting profit to $50,000 net partnership income because interest on drawings is removed from income and partner salaries/interest on capital are added back as non-deductible partnership expenses.','The distribution schedule then recognises agreed salaries and capital interest, subtracts interest on drawings, and distributes the remaining net income by partnership percentages. The slide’s repeated instruction is to distribute the net income, not accounting profit.'], checkpoint:'I can build the distribution schedule without accidentally distributing the accounting number.'},
      {title:'9. CGT, super and franked dividends flow through', kicker:'partner-level consequences', body:['Partnership assets are ultimately owned by partners for these purposes, so capital gains/losses on partnership assets are brought to account by the partners. Partner super contributions are claimed by the partners rather than the partnership.','Franked dividends received by the partnership enter partnership net income, including the franking credits, and the credits then flow to partners in proportion to their relevant share.'], checkpoint:'I can explain which tax attributes retain partner-level consequences.'},
      {title:'10. Reconciliation — accounting profit is only the starting point', kicker:'bridge to Topics 6–7', body:['The separate reconciliation deck applies to partnerships, trusts and companies. Start with accounting profit, reverse accounting items that are not treated the same way for tax, then insert the correct tax treatment.','The safest discipline is to reverse the full accounting entry first, then add/subtract the tax result. Trying to “net the difference” mentally is how signs get reversed.'], checkpoint:'I can reconcile partner salaries, accounting depreciation, provisions and tax-only items without mixing signs.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'Partnership accounting profit is $40,500 after deducting $15,000 partner salaries and $500 interest on partners’ capital, and after including $6,000 interest on drawings as income. Ordinary deductible expenses are otherwise correct. What adjustment direction gets you toward tax net income?', answer:'Remove the $6,000 interest-on-drawings accounting income, add back $15,000 partner salaries and $500 capital interest. In the Curtin example this produces $50,000 net partnership income.'},
      {q:'Does a partner’s $50,000 drawing cap their taxable partnership share at $50,000?', answer:'No. Drawings are advances of cash; assessment follows the partner’s share of net income.'},
      {q:'Partnership receives a franked dividend. Where do the franking credits ultimately go?', answer:'They are included in partnership net income and then flow to the partners in proportion to their share of the franked distribution, allowing partner-level offsets.'}
    ],
    finish:['Identify a partnership from TR 94/8-style facts.','Calculate s90 net income from accounting data.','Complete the full Curtin-style distribution schedule.','Explain drawings, partner salaries, capital interest and partner loans.','Reconcile one partnership accounting profit to net income.']
  },

  taxaRecon: {
    id:'taxaRecon', unit:'TAXA2000', unitName:'Introduction to Australian Taxation Law', date:'7–27 Sep', week:'8–10',
    title:'Tax Reconciliation Workshop — accounting profit to tax net income', duration:'75–110 min',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin Tax Reconciliations deck used across Partnerships, Trusts and Companies.',
    objectives:['Explain permanent and timing differences.','Reverse accounting entries and insert correct tax treatment.','Use the plus/minus reconciliation framework without sign errors.','Apply the approach to partnerships, trusts and companies.'],
    map:['accounting net profit','reverse accounting treatment','insert tax-only income','add back non-deductible accounting expenses','subtract non-tax accounting income','subtract tax deductions','taxable/net income'],
    sections:[
      {title:'1. Why reconciliation exists', kicker:'big picture', body:['Financial accounting and tax law answer different questions, so accounting profit does not automatically equal taxable/net income. Reconciliation converts the accounting result into the tax result without rebuilding every transaction from scratch.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why accounting profit is a starting point rather than a tax answer.'},
      {title:'2. Permanent differences', kicker:'never align', body:['Permanent differences arise where accounting and tax treatment differ in a way that does not reverse later. Course examples include franking credits or non-cash business benefits assessable for tax but absent from accounting income, exempt/pre-CGT amounts appearing in accounting profit but not taxable, non-deductible entertainment/fines/excessive payments, and tax-only deductions such as qualifying s40-880 expenditure.'], checkpoint:'I can identify a permanent difference from a timing difference.'},
      {title:'3. Timing differences', kicker:'same idea, different year', body:['Timing differences arise when accounting and tax recognise broadly the same economic item at different times. Provisions, accounting versus tax depreciation, disposal of depreciating assets, accruals and prepayments are central examples in the deck.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why a timing difference may reverse in a later year.'},
      {title:'4. Reverse first, then replace', kicker:'sign discipline', body:['If accounting income is not taxable, subtract the whole accounting income. If an accounting expense is not deductible, add the whole expense back. Then separately add taxable amounts or subtract tax deductions.','The slides explicitly warn against jumping straight to a net difference because it makes sign errors much easier.'], checkpoint:'I can articulate the accounting reversal and tax replacement as two separate steps.'},
      {title:'5. Standard reconciliation skeleton', kicker:'template', flow:['Accounting net profit','+ tax income missing from accounts','+ accounting expenses not deductible','− accounting income not taxable','− tax depreciation / Div43','− other tax deductions','Taxable / net income'], checkpoint:'I can recreate the skeleton without notes.'},
      {title:'6. Core examples from the deck', kicker:'application', bullets:['Accounting gain on sale $50,000 but discounted tax gain $25,000 → subtract accounting $50,000, add tax $25,000.','If active-asset reduction makes tax gain $12,500 → subtract accounting $50,000, add tax $12,500.','Franked dividend $14,000 + franking credit $6,000 → accounting may show $14,000; add the $6,000 tax-only gross-up.','SBE asset accounting depreciation $90 but tax write-off $900 → add back $90, subtract $900.','Leave provision $12,000 not currently deductible → add back $12,000.'], checkpoint:'I can decide both the reversal and replacement for each example.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'Accounting profit includes $20,000 exempt income. What is the reconciliation entry?', answer:'Subtract $20,000 because accounting income is not assessable for tax.'},
      {q:'Accounts deduct a $5,000 non-deductible fine. Reconciliation?', answer:'Add back the full $5,000 accounting expense.'},
      {q:'Accounting depreciation is $2,000, tax depreciation is $3,500. Reconciliation?', answer:'Add back $2,000 accounting depreciation, then subtract $3,500 tax depreciation.'}
    ],
    finish:['Build the reconciliation skeleton from memory.','Classify ten items permanent vs timing.','Complete one partnership/company reconciliation using reverse-then-replace.']
  },

  taxa6: {
    id:'taxa6', unit:'TAXA2000', unitName:'Introduction to Australian Taxation Law', date:'14–20 Sep', week:9,
    title:'Trusts & Minors — present entitlement, trust net income and Division 6AA', duration:'170–230 min',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin Topic 6/Module 7 Minors & Trust objectives and complete Minors deck. The uploaded Trust PDF was an incomplete .opdownload, so the missing Trust teaching layer is reconstructed from the Curtin objectives and current official ITAA 1936 Division 6 / ATO trust-streaming guidance. Any later lecturer transcript should refine, not erase, this base.',
    objectives:['Apply Division 6AA to prescribed minors and separate eligible from excepted income.','Identify basic trust roles and distinguish trust law income from tax net income.','Calculate trust net income and understand present entitlement.','Determine whether a beneficiary or trustee is assessed under the basic Division 6 framework.','Explain the course objective of streaming capital gains and franked distributions.','Recognise where the trust section is an official-source reconstruction because Curtin pages were incomplete.'],
    map:['trust earns income','calculate trust net income','determine trust income + beneficiary entitlement','present entitlement?','beneficiary vs trustee assessment','special character: capital gain / franked distribution','minor? → Div 6AA overlay'],
    sections:[
      {title:'1. What a trust is doing for tax purposes', kicker:'big picture · reconstructed trust section', body:['A trust separates legal control of property from the benefits enjoyed by beneficiaries. The trustee controls/administers trust property under the trust deed and trust law; beneficiaries may become entitled to income or capital according to those terms.','For income tax, the central problem is not simply “the trust earned $X”. You must calculate the trust estate’s tax net income and then determine who is assessed on it — a presently entitled beneficiary, the trustee on behalf of certain beneficiaries, or the trustee on income to which no beneficiary is presently entitled.'], callout:'The uploaded Curtin file did not contain the Trust teaching pages. This section follows the objectives visible in the Curtin Minors & Trust deck and fills the gap with current official legislation/ATO material.', checkpoint:'I can name trustee, trust estate and beneficiary and explain why the person assessed depends on entitlement.'},
      {title:'2. Trust income versus “net income of the trust estate”', kicker:'do not collapse the two', body:['Trust law/deed income is used to determine beneficiaries’ entitlements. Tax “net income of the trust estate” under s95 is a tax calculation broadly based on assessable income less allowable deductions as if the trustee were a resident taxpayer, with statutory modifications.','This distinction matters because a beneficiary’s proportionate entitlement to trust income can determine the share of tax net income assessed to them under the general Division 6 rules. A reconciliation may therefore be needed where accounting/trust income and tax treatment differ.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why trust distributable income and taxable net income are related but not necessarily identical.'},
      {title:'3. Present entitlement', kicker:'core trust concept', body:['Present entitlement is the pivot of the basic trust assessment rules. Section 95A includes rules that continue or deem present entitlement in specified circumstances. In practical course terms, ask whether the beneficiary has a current, vested right to demand or benefit from the relevant trust income under the deed/resolution.','Do not infer present entitlement merely because someone is named as a beneficiary. Discretionary beneficiaries often need a valid trustee resolution allocating income before year end to create the relevant entitlement.'], checkpoint:'I can distinguish being a possible beneficiary from being presently entitled to a share of income.'},
      {title:'4. Beneficiary assessed — basic s97 case', kicker:'adult resident beneficiary', body:['Under the current s97 framework, a beneficiary who is presently entitled and not under a legal disability includes the relevant share of the trust estate’s net income in assessable income, subject to the detailed statutory rules.','This is the basic flow-through result: trust earns/calculates net income, entitlement allocates the share, beneficiary is assessed.'], checkpoint:'I can identify the basic conditions that point to beneficiary assessment.'},
      {title:'5. Trustee assessed — legal disability or no present entitlement', kicker:'s98 / s99 / s99A map', body:['Where a beneficiary is presently entitled but under a legal disability, s98 can assess the trustee in respect of that beneficiary’s share. Where income is not matched to a presently entitled beneficiary, trustee assessment provisions such as s99 or s99A can become relevant depending on the facts.','For this introductory lesson, the important skill is routing the facts to the right assessment person before attempting rates. Detailed exceptions should be checked against the exam aid/lecturer examples when supplied.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why “trustee pays tax” is not a universal statement about trusts.'},
      {title:'6. Reconciliation still applies to trusts', kicker:'accounting → tax', body:['The Curtin reconciliation slides expressly list trusts alongside partnerships and companies. Start with accounting profit/net income, reverse accounting treatments that do not match tax, add tax-only assessable amounts and subtract tax deductions.','Examples that can matter include accounting depreciation versus tax depreciation, provisions, franking-credit gross-ups, net capital gains and tax-only deductions.'], checkpoint:'I can use the same reverse-then-replace reconciliation discipline for a trust.'},
      {title:'7. Streaming capital gains and franked distributions', kicker:'official ATO supplement', body:['The ATO explains that a trustee can stream capital gains or franked distributions where the trust deed gives the required power and the statutory requirements for specific entitlement are met. A beneficiary specifically entitled to the capital gain or franked distribution is generally assessed on that character and can receive associated tax attributes such as franking credits, subject to integrity rules.','The ATO also warns that other types of trust income cannot simply be streamed in the same character-preserving way. Treat “streaming” as a specific statutory mechanism, not a free-form decision to send any tax attribute to any beneficiary.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why the trust deed and specific entitlement matter to streaming.'},
      {title:'8. Minors — why Division 6AA exists', kicker:'Curtin source resumes here', body:['Division 6AA is designed to discourage income splitting by diverting passive/investment income to children. The deck applies it to a “prescribed person”: someone under 18 at 30 June who is not an excepted person.','The practical sequence is person first, then income type. Do not apply penalty rates to all income merely because the taxpayer is under 18.'], checkpoint:'I can test prescribed person status before classifying the minor’s income.'},
      {title:'9. Excepted persons', kicker:'s102AC', body:['The Curtin deck lists minors in full-time occupation, disabled minors and double orphans as excepted persons. If a minor is excepted, the special Division 6AA penalty regime does not apply in the ordinary way taught here.'], checkpoint:'I can identify when the person, rather than the income, is outside the penalty regime.'},
      {title:'10. Eligible versus excepted assessable income', kicker:'s102AE', body:['Eligible assessable income is the non-excepted income to which Division 6AA applies. The deck’s examples treat ordinary investment income gifted by a parent/family-trust distributions as eligible in the relevant facts.','Excepted assessable income includes employment/business income and specified investment income connected to damages awards, deceased estates or the reinvestment of excepted income. Excepted income is taxed at ordinary rates.'], table:[['Income','Course classification clue'],['part-time salary','excepted assessable income'],['interest on money gifted by parent','eligible assessable income'],['interest on inherited deceased-estate funds','excepted assessable income'],['ordinary family trust distribution to minor','eligible in the deck example']], checkpoint:'I can split one minor’s taxable income into ordinary-rate and Div6AA components.'},
      {title:'11. Division 6AA rates in the supplied deck', kicker:'course table', body:['The deck uses nil tax up to $416 of Division 6AA income, 66% of the excess over $416 for $417–$1,307, and 45% of the entire amount from $1,308 upward. It also states that LITO cannot be used to reduce tax on eligible assessable income.','Because tax rates can change, use the assessment-year exam aid if the lecturer later supplies a replacement table.'], checkpoint:'I can apply the correct band to eligible assessable income without mixing it with ordinary-rate income.'},
      {title:'12. Bronte example', kicker:'worked logic', body:['Bronte is 16, earns $10,000 salary and receives $4,000 interest on money deposited by her father. She is a prescribed person. Salary is excepted assessable income; the investment interest is eligible assessable income.','The deck therefore applies ordinary rates to salary and the Division 6AA rate to the $4,000 interest, producing $1,800 tax on the eligible component in the example.'], checkpoint:'I can explain the classification before quoting the $1,800 result.'},
      {title:'13. Leanne example', kicker:'inherited funds versus trust distribution', body:['Leanne is 17. Interest of $19,000 earned on funds left under her grandmother’s will is treated as excepted assessable income. A $3,000 family-trust distribution is treated as eligible assessable income.','The example applies the 45% Division 6AA rate to the $3,000 distribution, producing $1,350 tax on that component; the ordinary-rate component is calculated separately.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why two investment-looking receipts can be classified differently because of their source.'},
      {title:'14. Bringing trusts and minors together', kicker:'combined scenario', body:['A trust distribution to a minor requires two layers. First determine the trust-side entitlement/assessment outcome. Then, if the amount is included in the minor’s assessable income, classify it under Division 6AA as eligible or excepted assessable income.','This is the deeper lesson behind the deck title “Minors and Trust”: entity allocation rules and minor tax-rate rules can stack rather than replace one another.'], checkpoint:'I can separate the trust allocation question from the minor-rate question.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'An adult resident beneficiary is presently entitled to 40% of trust income and is not under legal disability. What is the basic Division 6 direction?', answer:'s97 points toward the beneficiary being assessed on the relevant share of the trust estate’s net income, subject to the detailed rules.'},
      {q:'A beneficiary is named in a discretionary trust deed but no valid distribution resolution gives them current entitlement. Is naming alone enough for present entitlement?', answer:'No. Present entitlement depends on the actual legal/deed entitlement, not merely being within the class of potential beneficiaries.'},
      {q:'Bronte, 16, earns $10,000 salary and $4,000 interest on money gifted by her father. Which component is eligible Div6AA income in the Curtin example?', answer:'The $4,000 interest. The salary is excepted assessable income.'},
      {q:'Can a trustee freely stream ordinary interest income to preserve any desired tax character just because the deed gives broad discretion?', answer:'No. The ATO guidance specifically supports character streaming for capital gains and franked distributions where statutory/deed requirements are met; other income does not have the same streaming regime.'}
    ],
    finish:['Explain trust parties, trust income and tax net income.','Route examples through s97/s98/no-present-entitlement trustee assessment at an introductory level.','Complete one trust reconciliation.','Explain capital-gain/franked-distribution streaming and specific entitlement.','Apply Division 6AA to both Curtin minor examples.','When a Trust lecture transcript arrives, compare its worked examples against this reconstructed section and update only where the lecturer adds/changes course-specific treatment.']
  },

  taxa7: {
    id:'taxa7', unit:'TAXA2000', unitName:'Introduction to Australian Taxation Law', date:'21–27 Sep', week:10,
    title:'Companies & Franking — company tax, private-company rules and dividend imputation', duration:'190–250 min',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin Companies lecture notes (34 pages), Companies Franking lecture notes (30 pages) and the tax-reconciliation deck.',
    objectives:['Calculate company taxable income and apply BRE/non-BRE rates taught in the course.','Distinguish public and private companies and apply key private-company rules.','Explain Division 7A-style deemed dividends and company loss continuity.','Calculate company PAYG instalment/net tax examples.','Explain dividend imputation, franking percentage and franking-account entries.','Apply benchmark and franking-deficit concepts.'],
    map:['company taxable income','company tax','private-company rules','after-tax profits','frank dividend','shareholder gross-up + credit','franking account / benchmark'],
    sections:[
      {title:'1. A company is its own taxpayer', kicker:'big picture', body:['Unlike a partnership, a company is a separate legal entity and taxable in its own right. Shareholders own the company and receive distributions as dividends; directors manage it on their behalf.','Calculate company taxable income in the familiar way: assessable income less allowable deductions. Resident-company income includes relevant Australian and foreign-source amounts under the course framework.'], checkpoint:'I can explain the structural tax difference between a partnership and company.'},
      {title:'2. BRE versus non-BRE', kicker:'company rate', body:['The lecture defines a base rate entity as having turnover below $50 million and base-rate passive income not more than 80% of assessable income. It applies a 25% company rate to a BRE and 30% to a non-BRE. Companies do not pay Medicare levy.'], checkpoint:'I can test BRE status before multiplying taxable income by a company rate.'},
      {title:'3. Public versus private company', kicker:'why classification matters', body:['The slides treat a listed company/public-company subsidiary under the public-company rules and define private company by exclusion. The practical reason this matters is that private companies face special rules for payments to associates and stricter loss/bad-debt rules.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why Pty Ltd facts should make me check private-company provisions.'},
      {title:'4. Excessive payments to associated persons', kicker:'s109 course rule', body:['If a private company pays an associated person an excessive amount purporting to be remuneration/allowance/retirement compensation, the excessive portion can be treated as a dividend.','In the lecture example, $50,000 paid to the managing director’s spouse when $10,000 is reasonable produces a $40,000 adjustment: company income increases by the excessive amount and the spouse has $10,000 salary plus a $40,000 dividend.'], checkpoint:'I can calculate the reasonable-payment versus deemed-dividend split.'},
      {title:'5. Division 7A — private-company value extracted by associates', kicker:'deemed dividends', body:['The course introduces Division 7A for private-company payments, loans and debt forgiveness involving associated entities. A loan not repaid by the relevant lodgment day can be deemed a dividend unless an exception such as a complying written loan applies.','The slide notes maximum terms of 25 years for qualifying property-secured loans and 7 years for other qualifying loans, and that a Division 7A deemed dividend is not frankable.'], checkpoint:'I can recognise a shareholder/associate loan as a tax issue rather than ordinary receivable bookkeeping.'},
      {title:'6. Company losses — COT then business continuity', kicker:'Division 165 course framework', body:['A company can deduct carried-forward losses if it satisfies the continuity-of-ownership test or, if that fails, the business-continuity test. The COT requires the same persons to beneficially own more than 50% of voting, dividend and capital rights through the ownership test period.','The lecture example follows the same 60% held by A and B through loss, intervening and profit years, allowing the loss to offset later profit.'], checkpoint:'I can test continuity across the whole ownership period, not just compare first and last year.'},
      {title:'7. Company PAYG instalments', kicker:'paying tax through the year', body:['The lecture shows company tax being prepaid through quarterly PAYG instalments, then reconciled against final tax payable. The key exam skill is identifying which instalments relate to the current income year — do not include a payment that actually settles the prior year.'], checkpoint:'I can separate current-year instalment credits from a prior-year payment.'},
      {title:'8. Company CGT difference', kicker:'no 50% general discount', body:['Companies do not receive the 50% general CGT discount. An eligible SBE company may still access the active-asset concession taught in Topic 4. In the slide example, a vacant-land gain receives no general discount while the business-asset gain can receive the active-asset reduction.'], checkpoint:'I will not automatically apply the individual 50% discount to a company.'},
      {title:'9. Why dividend imputation exists', kicker:'company tax passed to shareholder', body:['Company profits can be taxed once in the company and then distributed as dividends. The imputation system recognises company tax at shareholder level through a franking credit, so the shareholder is effectively taxed according to their own position after receiving credit for tax already paid by the company.','A fully franked cash dividend is grossed up by the franking credit in assessable income, then the credit is used as a tax offset.'], checkpoint:'I can explain imputation as integration of company/shareholder tax rather than a bonus payment.'},
      {title:'10. Maximum franking credits', kicker:'30/70 and 25/75', formulas:['Non-BRE maximum credit = frankable distribution × 30/70','BRE maximum credit = frankable distribution × 25/75'], body:['These fractions reverse-engineer the tax embedded in after-tax profit. A $70 distribution from profits taxed at 30% can carry up to $30 credit; a $75 distribution from profits taxed at 25% can carry up to $25.'], checkpoint:'I can derive the gross pre-tax profit from a fully franked cash distribution.'},
      {title:'11. The benchmark rule', kicker:'consistent franking percentage', body:['The first frankable distribution in a franking period establishes the benchmark franking percentage, and later frankable distributions in that period should use the same benchmark. The rule prevents selective favouring of shareholders through different franking percentages.','Franking below the benchmark creates a franking debit; franking above can produce over-franking tax under the course treatment.'], formulas:['franking % = allocated franking credit ÷ maximum franking credit × 100'], checkpoint:'I can calculate the first dividend’s benchmark percentage and test later dividends against it.'},
      {title:'12. Franking account — a tax ledger, not an accounting cash account', kicker:'Division 205', body:['The franking account records the company’s capacity to pass tax credits to shareholders. Credits generally arise when company tax/PAYG is paid and when franked distributions are received. Debits arise when franked dividends are paid, tax is refunded or certain benchmark consequences occur.','An opening surplus carries forward. The closing balance tells you whether the company still has franking capacity or has over-distributed credits.'], table:[['Typical event','Franking account direction'],['PAYG/income tax paid','credit'],['franked dividend received','credit for attached credit'],['franked dividend paid','debit'],['income-tax refund','debit'],['benchmark-rule debit','debit']], checkpoint:'I can post the direction of common events without confusing cash in/out with franking debits/credits.'},
      {title:'13. Franking deficit tax', kicker:'closing deficit', body:['If franking debits exceed credits at year end, the company has a franking deficit and pays franking deficit tax equal to the deficit. The lecture emphasises that FDT is not a penalty; it is effectively a prepayment that can be offset against future company income-tax liability under the relevant rules.'], checkpoint:'I can distinguish FDT from over-franking penalty/tax and explain why it resets the account.'},
      {title:'14. Reconciliation for companies', kicker:'accounting → taxable income', body:['A company question may start with accounting profit. Add back non-deductible accounting expenses and accounting depreciation, remove non-taxable accounting income, insert tax depreciation, net capital gains and franking-credit gross-ups, and apply other tax deductions.','The reconciliation is the bridge between financial accounts and the company taxable-income number on which 25%/30% is applied.'], checkpoint:'I can complete a company reconciliation before touching the company tax rate.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'A BRE company has taxable income of $200,000. Ignoring instalments, company tax under the course rate?', answer:'$50,000 at 25%.'},
      {q:'A non-BRE pays a fully franked cash dividend of $70. Maximum franking credit?', answer:'$30 using 70 × 30/70.'},
      {q:'A private company pays an associate $50,000 for work worth $10,000. Course excessive amount?', answer:'$40,000, potentially treated as a dividend under the private-company rule taught.'},
      {q:'Who gets the 50% general CGT discount: an individual shareholder or the company itself?', answer:'An eligible individual can; the company does not receive the 50% general discount.'},
      {q:'Franking account closes with debits exceeding credits by $8,000. What is the course consequence?', answer:'A franking deficit of $8,000 and FDT of $8,000 to bring the account back to nil.'}
    ],
    finish:['Calculate BRE/non-BRE company tax.','Work one COT ownership table.','Classify a private-company payment/loan scenario.','Gross up and credit a franked dividend.','Complete a franking-percentage and benchmark example.','Build a full franking account and determine surplus/deficit.','Complete one company tax reconciliation.']
  },

  taxa8: {
    id:'taxa8', unit:'TAXA2000', unitName:'Introduction to Australian Taxation Law', date:'5–11 Oct', week:12,
    title:'GST — taxable supplies, input tax credits and BAS logic', duration:'160–220 min',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin Topic 8 / Module 9 GST lecture notes (55 pages).',
    objectives:['Explain how GST moves through the supply chain and why the final consumer bears the cost.','Test whether a supply is taxable under s9-5.','Distinguish taxable, GST-free and input-taxed supplies.','Test GST registration and creditable acquisitions.','Calculate GST collected, input tax credits and net GST payable.','Understand the logic of reporting through the BAS.'],
    map:['supply','taxable?','GST on output','acquisition','creditable?','input tax credit','GST collected − credits','BAS / amount payable'],
    sections:[
      {title:'1. GST is a value-added consumption tax', kicker:'big picture', body:['GST is a 10% broad-based consumption tax collected through registered businesses at successive stages of the supply chain. Businesses claim credits for GST they pay on creditable acquisitions, so the economic burden is intended to reach the final consumer rather than accumulate as a tax on each business.','The timber → manufacturer → retailer → consumer example makes this visible: each business effectively remits GST on the value it adds, while the final consumer cannot claim an input tax credit.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why the consumer bears $30 GST on a $330 final sale even though several businesses make payments to the ATO.'},
      {title:'2. The three supply categories', kicker:'memorise the matrix', table:[['Supply type','Charge GST on output?','Input tax credits on related acquisitions?'],['Taxable','Yes','Yes'],['GST-free','No','Yes'],['Input taxed','No','No']], checkpoint:'I can state both output and input-credit consequences for all three categories.'},
      {title:'3. Taxable supply — s9-5 checklist', kicker:'seller perspective', bullets:['supply for consideration','made in course/furtherance of enterprise','connected with Australia','supplier registered or required to register','not GST-free or input taxed'], body:['All conditions need to be satisfied. Use the checklist in order so you do not calculate 1/11 GST on a transaction that is outside the taxable-supply rules.'], checkpoint:'I can run a transaction through all s9-5 elements before calculating GST.'},
      {title:'4. What counts as a supply?', kicker:'s9-10 broad definition', body:['Supply is deliberately broad: goods, services, advice/information, real property, rights, financial supplies and entering/releasing obligations can all be supplies. A single commercial transaction can contain several supplies, so classification may require separating components.','Unlawful transactions can still be supplies for GST purposes; legality is not the threshold test for whether GST concepts apply.'], checkpoint:'I can recognise non-physical services/rights as supplies.'},
      {title:'5. Consideration', kicker:'s9-15', body:['Consideration is what is given in exchange for the supply and is broader than cash. Barter and third-party payment arrangements can still provide consideration. Transactions between associates can trigger special market-value rules.'], checkpoint:'I will not conclude “no consideration” merely because no cash changed hands directly.'},
      {title:'6. Enterprise', kicker:'s9-20', body:['The enterprise concept includes activities carried on as a business, an adventure/concern in the nature of trade, regular leasing/licensing and certain charitable/religious/government activities. The slides exclude hobbies/recreation, some activities without reasonable profit expectation and employee activities.'], checkpoint:'I can distinguish an employee earning wages from an enterprise making supplies.'},
      {title:'7. Connected with Australia', kicker:'s9-25', body:['The course connects goods with Australia when they are delivered/made available/imported/installed here; Australian real property is connected here; other supplies can be connected when done in Australia or supplied through an enterprise carried on in Australia.'], checkpoint:'I can identify which factual connection matters for goods, real property or services.'},
      {title:'8. Registration', kicker:'who enters the GST system', body:['Registered entities must charge/collect GST on taxable supplies and can claim input tax credits on creditable acquisitions. Registration therefore determines both obligations and entitlements.','Use the lecture/exam aid for turnover thresholds and special registration rules for the assessment year rather than relying on memory from another context.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why registration affects both GST collected and credits claimed.'},
      {title:'9. Creditable acquisitions — buyer perspective', kicker:'input tax credit test', body:['After testing the seller’s supply, switch perspective. A buyer generally needs a creditable purpose, a taxable supply made to them, consideration and registration to claim an input tax credit. Private/non-creditable portions are excluded or apportioned.'], checkpoint:'I can keep “taxable supply” and “creditable acquisition” as separate but linked tests.'},
      {title:'10. Calculating GST in inclusive prices', kicker:'arithmetic', formulas:['GST on GST-inclusive taxable price = price × 1/11','GST-exclusive price × 10% = GST','GST payable = GST collected − input tax credits'], checkpoint:'I can move between inclusive, exclusive and GST amounts without adding 10% twice.'},
      {title:'11. Supply-chain worked logic', kicker:'timber → table', body:['The timber merchant sells for $110 including $10 GST and remits $10. The manufacturer buys for $110, claims the $10 input credit, sells for $220 including $20 GST and therefore nets $10 to the ATO. The retailer claims $20, sells for $330 including $30 GST and nets another $10.','Across the chain the ATO receives $30, exactly the GST embedded in the consumer’s final $330 price.'], checkpoint:'I can reconcile individual-business GST with the total tax borne by the consumer.'},
      {title:'12. GST-free versus input taxed', kicker:'do not confuse “no GST charged”', body:['Both categories normally have no GST charged on the output, but their input-credit treatment is opposite. GST-free suppliers can generally claim credits for related creditable acquisitions; input-taxed suppliers generally cannot.','That difference is often more important than the fact that the customer sees no GST line on the supply.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why GST-free can be more favourable to the supplier than input taxed.'},
      {title:'13. BAS logic', kicker:'reporting', body:['The Business Activity Statement brings the period’s GST collected and input tax credits together with other reported tax obligations. For this module, the central GST calculation is output GST less allowable input tax credits, producing a net amount payable or credit/refund position.'], checkpoint:'I can build the GST component of a simple BAS from a transaction list.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'A taxable sale is $1,100 GST-inclusive. GST component?', answer:'$100 using 1,100 × 1/11.'},
      {q:'A GST-free supply: GST charged on output? Input credits on qualifying related acquisitions?', answer:'No GST on output; input tax credits generally remain available.'},
      {q:'An input-taxed supply: output GST and related input credits?', answer:'No output GST and generally no input tax credits on related acquisitions.'},
      {q:'Business collects $8,000 GST and has $5,500 allowable input tax credits. Net GST?', answer:'$2,500 payable before considering any other BAS items.'}
    ],
    finish:['Memorise the three-supply matrix.','Apply s9-5 to multiple scenarios.','Apply the creditable-acquisition test from buyer perspective.','Complete inclusive/exclusive GST calculations.','Build one simple BAS GST reconciliation.']
  },

  taxa9: {
    id:'taxa9', unit:'TAXA2000', unitName:'Introduction to Australian Taxation Law', date:'12–18 Oct', week:13,
    title:'Tax Practitioners — registration, services, conduct and liability', duration:'100–145 min',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin Module 10 Tax Practitioners / Tax Agents lecture notes (35 pages).',
    objectives:['Explain the Tax Practitioners Board’s role.','Identify the broad requirements for tax-agent registration.','Explain fit-and-proper, education and experience requirements in the course material.','Identify a tax agent service and unregistered-practice risks.','Apply the Code of Professional Conduct categories.','Describe TPB sanctions, civil penalties and professional liability.'],
    map:['TASA 2009','TPB registration','fit & proper + education + experience','tax agent service','Code duties','investigation / sanctions','civil or negligence liability'],
    sections:[
      {title:'1. Why tax practitioners are regulated', kicker:'big picture', body:['Clients and the tax system rely on advisers to apply complex law accurately. The Tax Agent Services Act 2009 establishes the registration and conduct framework administered by the Tax Practitioners Board.','The TPB registers agents, supports competency requirements, investigates complaints and acts against unregistered entities holding themselves out as registered practitioners.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why practitioner regulation protects both clients and tax administration.'},
      {title:'2. Registration requirements', kicker:'gateway', body:['The course lists three broad pillars: fit and proper person, minimum educational qualifications and relevant experience. The Board can also require professional indemnity insurance. Registration can be held by individuals, partnerships or companies; trust structures require the registered entity/trustee arrangement described in the material.'], checkpoint:'I can list the registration pillars without confusing them with Code duties after registration.'},
      {title:'3. Fit and proper person', kicker:'judgment + trustworthiness', body:['The fit-and-proper test is case-specific and involves discretionary judgment. The lecture’s case extract frames fitness around honesty, knowledge and ability — reputation, tax-law knowledge, competence in return preparation and dealings with the revenue authority.','Treat it as a holistic professional suitability test, not merely “has no criminal record”.'], checkpoint:'I can apply honesty, competence and reputation factors to a scenario.'},
      {title:'4. Education and relevant experience', kicker:'course pathways', body:['The slides present several pathways: relevant tertiary qualification plus experience, diploma plus longer experience, or substantial experience without formal qualifications/professional-association pathways. The exact combination depends on the regulatory category.','Relevant experience includes work as/under supervision of a registered agent, legal practice or other TPB-approved work with substantial tax-agent-service involvement.'], callout:'Registration fees, experience periods and regulator terminology can change. For an assessment, follow the current Curtin/TPB material supplied for that year.', checkpoint:'I can explain what makes work experience “relevant” rather than merely accounting-adjacent.'},
      {title:'5. Notification obligations', kicker:'ongoing registration', body:['A registered agent must notify the Board within the course-specified period when they cease to satisfy registration requirements or a relevant event occurs. Registration is therefore an ongoing obligation, not a one-off credential.'], checkpoint:'I can explain why post-registration changes matter to the TPB.'},
      {title:'6. What is a tax agent service?', kicker:'two-part definition', body:['The first element is subject matter: ascertaining tax liabilities/obligations/entitlements, advising about them, or representing an entity in dealings with the Commissioner. The second is reliance: the client can reasonably be expected to rely on the service to satisfy obligations or claim entitlements.','Examples include preparing/lodging returns and objections, advising on tax law, review/appeal assistance and dealing with the Commissioner for a taxpayer.'], checkpoint:'I can test both subject matter and client reliance before calling something a tax agent service.'},
      {title:'7. Unregistered practice and civil penalties', kicker:'who can charge for services', body:['An unregistered entity can breach the civil-penalty provisions by knowingly providing a tax agent service for fee/reward, advertising such services or representing that it is registered. Registered agents can also face civil penalties for serious false/misleading statements and improper use of deregistered/preparer arrangements.'], checkpoint:'I can identify the difference between registration sanctions and Federal Court civil penalties.'},
      {title:'8. Code of Professional Conduct — five themes', kicker:'ethical framework', body:['The lecture groups the Code into honesty/integrity, independence, confidentiality, competence and other responsibilities. Application questions should link conduct to a specific duty rather than merely saying “unethical”.'], table:[['Theme','Examples from course'],['Honesty & integrity','act honestly; comply with own tax obligations; account for client money'],['Independence','act lawfully in client best interests; manage conflicts'],['Confidentiality','do not disclose client affairs without permission unless legally required'],['Competence','competent service; maintain skills; reasonable care on facts and law'],['Other responsibilities','do not obstruct tax administration; advise rights/obligations; maintain required PII; respond to TPB']], checkpoint:'I can identify which Code theme a factual breach belongs to.'},
      {title:'9. TPB sanctions for Code breaches', kicker:'administrative consequences', body:['The Board can caution an agent, require training, impose practising restrictions, require supervision, suspend registration or terminate registration. Severity depends on the nature and circumstances of the breach.'], checkpoint:'I can describe a range of sanctions rather than assuming every breach means immediate deregistration.'},
      {title:'10. Investigations', kicker:'TPB process', body:['The TPB can require documents/information and witness attendance when investigating registration or conduct issues. The lecture describes timeframes for making investigation decisions and the consequences if no decision is made in time.'], checkpoint:'I can explain the Board’s evidence-gathering role.'},
      {title:'11. Negligence and common-law liability', kicker:'client can also sue', body:['Regulatory compliance does not eliminate civil liability to clients. Tax agents can be sued for breach of contract, negligence or fiduciary duties when the legal elements are met. The lecture cases emphasise misuse of client money, failure to follow instructions, failure to advise on relevant law and the need to exercise independent professional care rather than blindly accepting client information.','A claimant still needs to prove that the practitioner’s breach caused loss.'], checkpoint:'I can distinguish TPB discipline from a client’s negligence action.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'Someone prepares tax returns for a fee and advertises themselves as a tax agent but is not registered. What broad issue?', answer:'Potential breach of the TASA civil-penalty rules for unregistered tax agent services/holding out.'},
      {q:'An agent discloses a client’s tax position to a friend without permission or legal duty. Which Code theme?', answer:'Confidentiality.'},
      {q:'An agent gives advice without checking obvious missing facts. Which Code theme?', answer:'Competence/reasonable care in ascertaining the client’s state of affairs.'},
      {q:'Does a TPB Code sanction prevent the client from also bringing a negligence claim?', answer:'No. Regulatory sanctions and common-law civil liability are separate avenues.'}
    ],
    finish:['Explain TPB/TASA structure.','Apply registration requirements to a hypothetical applicant.','Classify several services as tax agent services or not.','Apply all five Code themes to scenarios.','Distinguish TPB sanctions, civil penalties and client negligence claims.']
  }
});
